'''Ames Uravan''' was a [[human]] [[Gender|male]] [[scientist]] and [[engineer]] from the [[Outer Rim Territories|Outer Rim]] [[planet]] [[Christophsis]]. A pioneer in the [[kyber crystal]] research field, Uravan was forcibly conscripted into [[Galactic Empire|Imperial]] service around [[19 BBY]]. During that time, he was assigned to the [[kyber crystal research team]] division of the [[Tarkin Initiative]], which was stationed at the [[Eadu Energy Conversion Laboratory]] on the [[mountain]]ous Outer Rim planet [[Eadu]]. Under the leadership of the [[Crystallography|crystallographer]] [[Galen Walton Erso|Galen Erso]], Uravan and his colleagues [[Project Stardust|worked]] to turn kyber [[crystal]]s into a viable energy source for the [[Mk I Superlaser|superlaser]] of the [[DS-1 Orbital Battle Station|Death Star]] [[battle station]], giving it the capability to destroy entire planets. Uravan's duties on Eadu included synthetically fusing together kyber crystal fragments [[Kyber crystal container|imported]] from the [[Mid Rim Territories|Mid Rim]] [[moon]] [[Jedha]].

In [[1 BBY]], Imperial [[Director]] [[Orson Callan Krennic|Orson Krennic]] found evidence that a member of Uravan's research team had [[Treason|conspired]] with a [[Bodhi Rook|cargo pilot]] to send a message to the [[Alliance to Restore the Republic|Rebel Alliance]] about the [[Death Star]] project. Krennic brought [[death trooper]]s to confront Uravan and his colleagues, instructing the traitor to expose himself. Despite Erso admitting to the betrayal, Krennic proceeded to have the [[trooper]]s [[Death|execute]] Uravan and the rest of his research team.

==Biography==
===Imperial service===
[[File:TarkinInitiative.svg|thumb|left|215px|While in Imperial service, Ames Uravan worked for a division of the Tarkin Initiative (insignia pictured).]]
A [[Homeworld|native]] of<ref name="ROUVG" /> the [[Outer Rim Territories]] [[planet]]<ref name="Databank">{{Databank|christophsis|Christophsis}}</ref> [[Christophsis]],<ref name="ROUVG" /> Ames Uravan was a gifted [[scientist]] and [[engineer]]<ref name="Geektionary" /> who pioneered [[phase-shifted grafting technique]]s in the fields of crystalline engineering<ref name="ROUVG" /> [[kyber crystal]] research. He<ref name="Geektionary" /> was forcibly conscripted into the service of the [[Galactic Empire]]<ref name="ROUVG" /> around [[19 BBY]]<ref name="Date" /> to serve as a specialist<ref name="Geektionary" /> on the [[kyber crystal research team]], a division of the [[Tarkin Initiative]] managed by [[Director]] [[Orson Callan Krennic|Orson Krennic]].<ref name="ROUVG" /> Identification records of the scientists working for Krennic,<ref name="Dossier">''[[Star Wars: Rogue One: Rebel Dossier]]''</ref> including Uravan,<ref name="ROUVG" /> were deleted from public databases upon their admittance to Imperial service.<ref name="Dossier" />

Uravan and his colleagues were stationed in the Outer Rim planet [[Eadu]]'s [[Eadu Energy Conversion Laboratory|Energy Conversion Laboratory]], where they were kept secluded in [[Laboratory|laboratories]]<ref name="ROUVG" /> hidden by the world's [[mountain]]ous terrain.<ref name="SW AB">''[[Star Wars: Absolutely Everything You Need to Know, Updated and Expanded]]''</ref> The scientists [[Project Stardust|worked]] to make kyber [[crystal]]s a viable energy source for the [[Mk I Superlaser|superlaser]] of the [[DS-1 Orbital Battle Station|Death Star]], a [[Geonosian]]-designed [[battle station]] that would, with the [[superlaser]], possess the capability to destroy entire planets. While on Eadu, Uravan's duties included overseeing the [[Kyber crystal container|shipments]] of kyber crystal fragments from the [[Mid Rim Territories|Mid Rim]] [[moon]] [[Jedha]]. Using a phase-shifted grafting technique, he fused the fragments together synthetically,<ref name="ROUVG" /> taking care to preserve their delicate qualities.<ref name="Geektionary" /> The kyber crystal research team's [[Standard day|day]]-to-day needs on Eadu were managed by [[Sirro Argonne]], its operations manager.<ref name="ROUVG" />

By [[13 BBY]],<ref name="Timelines">''[[Star Wars: Timelines]]'' dates [[Galen Walton Erso]]'s [[Capture of Galen Erso|capture]] on [[Lah'mu]] to [[13 BBY]].</ref> work on the superlaser had stalled<ref name="RO NOVEL">[[Rogue One: A Star Wars Story (novelization)|''Rogue One: A Star Wars Story'' novelization]]</ref> and left the project [[Standard year|years]] behind schedule,<ref name="Rise">''[[Star Wars: The Rise and Fall of the Galactic Empire]]''</ref> prompting Krennic to [[Capture of Galen Erso|abduct]] his former colleague, the [[Crystallography|crystallographer]] [[Galen Walton Erso|Galen Erso]], and bring him into Imperial service. Erso's extraordinary intellect managed to revitalize the division's progress,<ref name="RO NOVEL" /> and Uravan and his team were able to complete the [[Death Star]]'s superlaser after several years under his leadership.<ref name="ROUVG" /> By [[1 BBY]],<ref name="Date" /> Uravan held the title of [[doctor]].<ref name="ROUVG" />

===Executed on Eadu===
{{Quote|Gentlemen, one of you betrayed the Empire. One of you has conspired with a pilot to send messages to the Rebellion. And I urge that traitor to step forward.|Director Orson Krennic, confronting Ames Uravan and his colleagues|audio=KrennicScientistsEadu.flac|<ref name="RO" />}}
[[File:GalenErsoAndFellowScientists-RogueOne3.png|thumb|right|180px|Ames Uravan (center) and his colleagues learn about Krennic's unplanned visit.]]
In 1 [['ABY-BBY' dating system|BBY]],<ref name="Date" /> [[Grand Moff]] [[Wilhuff Tarkin]] informed Director Krennic that a [[Alliance to Restore the Republic|rebel]] [[Treason|sympathizer]] within Erso's research team had conspired with a [[Bodhi Rook|cargo pilot]] to deliver a message to the Rebel Alliance concerning the Death Star. Krennic traveled to Eadu with a [[Krennic's guard squadron|group]] of [[death trooper]]s to investigate,<ref name="RO" /> informing the laboratory's workers, including Uravan, of his [[ST-149|shuttle's]] impending arrival.<ref name="RO 3">[[Rogue One Adaptation 3|''Rogue One Adaptation'' 3]]</ref> [[Stormtrooper]]s ushered the scientists out of the laboratories<ref name="RO NOVEL" /> to near the entrance of Krennic's [[Delta-class T-3c shuttle|shuttle]].<ref name="RO JUNIOR">''[[Star Wars: Rogue One: A Junior Novel]]''</ref> The director then assembled his death [[trooper]] unit around the scientists, recalling them each by name, including Uravan.<ref name="RO NOVEL" />

Krennic announced his knowledge of the conspiracy against the Empire and instructed the traitor to expose himself. The director received no response in the moments afterward and concluded that the scientists had conspired in a group effort. As Krennic's death troopers readied themselves to [[Death penalty|execute]] the scientists, Erso intervened and confessed to being the traitor in an attempt to save his team. However, as soon as Erso stepped out of the way, Uravan and his colleagues were shot and killed by the death troopers per Krennic's orders.<ref name="RO" />

Uravan's work contributed to the completion of the Death Star project,<ref name="RO NOVEL" /> although the [[space station]] was [[Battle of Yavin|destroyed]] soon after his death when the Rebel Alliance targeted a faulty [[thermal exhaust port]]<ref name="Geektionary" /> that Erso had deliberately planted inside.<ref name="RO NOVEL" /> In [[35 ABY]],<ref name="RiseDate">{{RiseAndFallDate}}</ref> the [[historian]] [[Beaumont Kin]] negatively compared Uravan's team to Erso in<ref name="Rise" /> his<ref name="TROSVD">''[[Star Wars: The Rise of Skywalker: The Visual Dictionary]]''</ref> [[book]] ''[[The Rise and Fall of the Galactic Empire]]''.<ref name="Rise" />

==Personality and traits==
{{Quote|In Erso's absence Krennic was forced to try and persevere with his other captive scientists, none of whom possessed the raw intellect of the man he had lost.|Beaumont Kin, ''The Rise and Fall of the Galactic Empire''|<ref name="Rise" />}}
Ames Uravan's forced conscription into Imperial service resulted in the passion for his crystal-centered endeavors to weaken.<ref name="ROUVG" /> The time spent in the kyber crystal research team's laboratories on Eadu left him frail and unused to the outdoors by the time of his death.<ref name="RO JUNIOR" /> Although Uravan and Krennic were acquaintances, the director felt no particular fondness toward him. Uravan was terrified at the prospect of death when confronted by Krennic's death troopers and was relieved when Erso stepped in to save his team, although it proved a futile gesture.<ref name="RO NOVEL" /> In his book, Kin expressed his opinion that neither Uravan or any of Krennic's other captive scientists possessed Erso's "raw intellect."<ref name="Rise" /> Uravan was a [[human]]<ref name="ROUVG" /> [[Gender|male]]<ref name="RO" /> who had [[Color|light]] skin, brown [[eye]]s, and brown [[hair]].<ref name="ROUVG" /> He was around 1.83 [[meter]]s tall.<ref name="Height" />

==Skills and abilities==
Uravan was a skilled scientist able to complete a phase-shifted grafting technique to connect kyber crystal fragments together synthetically.<ref name="ROUVG" /> His precision allowed for the crystals' delicate qualities to be preserved.<ref name="Geektionary" />

==Equipment==
[[File:EaduScientists.png|thumb|left|250px|On Eadu, Uravan (center) wore a blue-and-white engineering jumpsuit.]]
As a member of the kyber crystal research team, Uravan wore a blue-and-white, long-sleeved engineering [[jumpsuit]].<ref name="RO NOVEL" /> The [[Clothing|jumpsuit's]] [[antiseptic coveralls]] sported the dark-gray [[Imperial crest|insignia]] of the Empire on the left sleeve.<ref name="ROUVG" /> The fractured kyber-crystal&ndash;inspired<ref name="SW AB" /> logo of the Tarkin Initiative was present on the chest of Uravan's coveralls.<ref name="ROUVG" />

His uniform included a tunic that would change colors when in the presence of dangerous [[radiation]] and shoes that were grounded against potential static charges present within laboratories. His jumpsuit included a chest pocket that held two [[code cylinder]]s [[Programmer|programmed]] with access to restricted areas.<ref name="ROUVG" />

==Behind the scenes==
{{Quote|For the Visual Guide, gave a bunch of these scientists names that were basically phonemes made up of Los Alamos scientists' names chopped up, tossed into a blender and stuck together again.|Pablo Hidalgo|<ref name="Twitter">{{Twitter|username=pabl0hidalgo|url=status/1727942382644256970|name=[[Pablo Hidalgo]]|date=2023-11-24|quote=For the Visual Guide, gave a bunch of these scientists names that were basically phonemes made up of Los Alamos scientists' names chopped up, tossed into a blender and stuck together again.|archivefile=File:PabloLosAlamosTweet.png}}</ref>}}
[[File:EaduScientistsConcept.jpg|thumb|right|300px|Jon McCoy's concept artwork of the Eadu scientists' deaths for ''Rogue One: A Star Wars Story''.]]
Ames Uravan first appeared in the [[Star Wars Anthology Series|Anthology]] film ''[[Rogue One: A Star Wars Story]]'',<ref name="RO" /> as well as in its [[Rogue One: A Star Wars Story (novelization)|adult]]<ref name="RO NOVEL" /> and [[Star Wars: Rogue One: A Junior Novel|junior novelizations]],<ref name="RO JUNIOR" /> which released the same day as the film on [[December 16]], [[2016]].<ref name="SW.com">{{SW|url=news/rebel-reading-rogue-one-books|text=Rebel Reading: A Guide to ''Rogue One'' Books|author=[[James Floyd|Floyd, James]]|date=2016-12-14}}</ref> The scientist was portrayed by actor [[Robert Benedetti-Hall]].<ref name="RO" /> He was first debuted visually in a trailer for the film released on the official ''[[Star Wars]]'' [[Wikipedia:YouTube|YouTube]] channel on [[October 13]] of the same year.<ref name="Trailer">{{SWYouTube|sC9abcLLQpI|Rogue One: A Star Wars Story Trailer #2 (Official)|sw_url=video/rogue-one-a-star-wars-story-trailer-2}}</ref>

Although the film's credits refer to him as "Engineer,"<ref name="RO" /> Uravan's name was revealed in both ''[[Star Wars: Rogue One: The Ultimate Visual Guide]]'', an accompanying reference book written by [[Pablo Hidalgo]],<ref name="ROUVG" /> and in ''[[Star Wars: Rogue One: Secret Mission]]'', a [[Dorling Kindersley|DK Readers]] title authored by [[Jason Fry]].<ref name="ROSM">''[[Star Wars: Rogue One: Secret Mission]]''</ref> Both were released alongside the film on December 16.<ref name="SW.com" /> Hidalgo created several of the scientists' names by combining elements from the names of the real-life scientists associated with the [[Wikipedia:Los Alamos National Laboratory|Los Alamos National Laboratory]].<ref name="Twitter" /> For ''Rogue One'', concept artwork of the scientists' death scene was created by artist [[Jon McCoy]].<ref name="Artbook">''[[The Art of Rogue One: A Star Wars Story]]''</ref> Uravan was pictured in a card made as a promo for the ''[[Secrets of Power]]'' set of the [[Fantasy Flight Games]] [[Star Wars: Unlimited|''Star Wars'': Unlimited]] card game<ref name="SWU">{{SWU|set=Secrets of Power|cardname=Galen Erso|subtitle=You'll Never Win|type=PQ Participation|link=4229452447}}</ref> that was released on [[November 7]], [[2025]].<ref name="SSU">{{UnlimitedWeb|url=products/set-6-secrets-of-power|text=Set 6 Secrets of Power}}</ref> The card's artwork was drawn by [[Axel Hutt]] in [[2024]].<ref name="Axel">{{Bluesky|smirt.bsky.social|post/3mdlzt3vl522j|[[Axel Hutt]]|date=2026-01-29|quote="Galen Erso" for Star Wars Unlimited. I drew that in 2024.
Also, abolish ICE.|archiveurl=https://ghostarchive.org/archive/CXVcW}}</ref>

